              <h3>Coding professional - Backend developer</h3>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="uc_u1_s2_p3_interview_backend_dev" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="uc_u1_s2_p3_interview_backend_dev undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/uc_u1_s2_p3_interview_backend_dev.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/uc_u1_s2_p3_interview_backend_dev.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Leah:</strong> Being a backend developer can be very fulfilling and they're constantly updating their knowledge and learning new skills. It can also be rewarding financially as it pays well. The role involves a lot of problem solving and suits someone who is methodical and enjoys logical challenges. Work however can often overrun the usual working hours as when problems arise they must be dealt with and may not get resolved before the end of the working day.</p>
<p>Let's hear from a backend developer to learn more about what the role involves...</p>
<p><strong>Tom:</strong> I'm Tom, I'm currently working as a contract backend developer. In terms of qualifications, I've got a bachelor's degree in computer programming. Before I started contracting as a backend developer, I spent three years working as a junior web developer. Some people study for a master's degree and go straight into backend development, but if you've got the programming experience, you can do it even without a degree if you can show that you've got the experience.</p>
<p>I've been working as a backend developer for about three years. Initially started working for a web design company as a junior web developer where I was working on developing frontend platforms using languages like HTML5 and JavaScript, and they were really useful for learning about integration before I transitioned to backend development.</p>
<p>I also learned quite a lot of different programming techniques, which were useful for backend development, like writing APIs and SQL databases. And I really enjoyed that aspect of it.</p>
<p>I do enjoy being a backend developer. So I really enjoy writing code and sort of setting up servers, providing services for user interfaces and things like that. And if you're creative and you enjoy continually developing new skills, then it's a really good job to do because it's really satisfying at the end of it to be able to see what you put time into then working. It's also interesting to see how projects develop over time with continuous integration and development practices.</p>
<p>Responsibilities vary depending on what the job is. But it's often developing and maintaining code that drives applications from behind the user interface. So that's where it's really useful to know something about frontend development, because with backend development, you're essentially translating the user input on the frontend side and into the desired result or correctly storing data behind it. So in general, some of the responsibilities include adhering to GDPR rules and principles when you're using/storing data and making sure that that's secure. Implementing security measures and keeping the data you've collected safe, again, similar to GDPR. And creating and writing code libraries for the scaling of the web application and debugging these. It's also really important to be on top of the technology that's being implemented in your applications to make sure that it's the most up to date. You have to communicate well with the client. Keep on top of sprint meetings and just generally keep the client happy, really.</p>
<p>Because I work as a contractor, quite often I'm working remote so hours can vary a bit, but you do have to be responsive to emails and normal working times and you know, attending meetings when it's necessary. So for example, typically the day might start with a short meeting with the client or with colleagues that you're working with to sort of discuss recent issues and how they'll be resolved and then move on to work on whatever's the highest priority that you have to do. So that might be urgent bug fixes or fixing new issues that have arisen. Typically using languages like Java, Python, PHP, Ruby, or C#. And just ensuring that you're keeping the application optimised in terms of scaling and speed really. Yeah, since I'm working [in] contracting, often the timescales are quite short, so you occasionally have to put in extra hours, but generally it's quite satisfying to see the end result.</p>
<p>Keeping on top of the meetings with, like, collaboration tools, like GitHub for code or Slack for agile and attending meetings.</p>
<p>It's a good role for somebody who's sort of interested code and sort of application and structures. And it can be quite challenging, but at the same time, you sort of have to be quite innovative for it to solve some of the problems, which can be quite challenging, but also quite interesting. So I think that it's good if you've, if you've got that sort of you know, creative pursuit, basically.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
